1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Last time, we launched an agent with Query.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Now, let's see what actually happens after you call it.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Claude doesn't just answer one time, it runs a loop.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Reasoning, calling tools,

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
reading the results, and going again.

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This lesson walks through the five steps of that loop

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and how it knows when to stop.

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's break it down.

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's walk the whole loop once, top to bottom.

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
It starts when Claude receives your prompt,

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,000
along with the system prompt,

13
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
the tool definitions, and any history.

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
At that moment, the SDK yields an init message,

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
marking the run as open.

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Claude then responds.

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Sometimes that's plain text,

18
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
but often it's a request to call its tools.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The SDK executes those tools

20
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
and feeds the results back to Claude.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Now the cycle repeats.

22
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Claude reads the results, reasons again,

23
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
and may call more tools.

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
This continues until Claude answers

25
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
with no tool call at all.

26
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
That tool-free answer is the signal the loop is done.

27
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Now let's nail down the word turn,

28
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
because people trip on it.

29
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
A turn is a single round trip.

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Claude requests some tools, the SDK runs them,

31
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
and the results feed back,

32
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
all without handing control to your code in between.

33
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
That whole exchange counts as one turn.

34
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The turn ends when its tool results come back.

35
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,000
If Claude looks at those results

36
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
and decides to call more tools,

37
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
that's the next turn beginning.

38
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
So turns keep stacking

39
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
as long as Claude keeps reaching for tools.

40
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The loop only ends when Claude replies

41
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
with no tool call at all.

42
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
That final tool-free message

43
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
is the answer you were waiting for.

44
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
The loop speaks to your code through five typed messages,

45
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,000
so it helps to know each one.

46
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
A system message opens the run,

47
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and its init subtype is the very first thing you see.

48
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
An assistant message carries Claude's output,

49
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
both its text and any tool calls it wants to make.

50
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
A user message delivers the tool results

51
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
back into the loop,

52
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
closing that round trip.

53
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,000
A stream event shows up

54
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
only when you turn on partial streaming,

55
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
giving you token-by-token deltas,

56
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
and a result message closes the run,

57
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
carrying the total cost, the usage, and the final status.

58
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Learn these five, and the stream stops looking like noise.

59
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
It reads like a transcript of the loop.

60
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
So what stops a loop that never produces a tool-free answer? You bound it.

61
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Max turns limits how many tool-using turns the loop may take.

62
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Max budget USD limits how many dollars a run may spend.

63
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
That budget is checked between calls,

64
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
so actual spend can overshoot the cap

65
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
by up to one call's worth.

66
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Cross either one, and the loop halts,

67
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
and hands back an error result

68
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
that tells you which limit you hit.

69
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Here's the part that surprises people.

70
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
By default, there is no cap at all.

71
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Left unset, a run can loop

72
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
as long as Claude keeps calling tools.

73
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
So setting a bound is on you, the developer.

74
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
On anything autonomous or expensive,

75
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
a sensible max turns is the difference

76
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
between a controlled agent and a runaway one.

77
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Let's read that result message

78
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
because it's where a run reports for duty.

79
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Every result message carries a subtype.

80
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Success means the run finished cleanly,

81
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
and the error subtypes tell you exactly

82
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
which limit ended it, turns, or budget.

83
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Alongside the subtype, you get accounting,

84
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
total cost USD for the dollar cost,

85
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
usage for the tokens, num turns for how many turns ran,

86
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
and session ID if you want to resume later.

87
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Here's the catch worth memorizing.

88
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Only the success subtype carries the final result text.

89
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
On an error subtype, that result field is empty.

90
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
So if you read the result text

91
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
without checking the subtype first,

92
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
you can quietly get nothing back and never notice.

93
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Let's put the two outcomes side by side

94
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
because your code has to handle both.

95
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
A success result is the happy path.

96
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Its subtype is success,

97
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
and it carries the final result text

98
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
along with the cost, the usage, and the turn counts.

99
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
An error result comes from a subtype like error max turns

100
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
or error max budget USD.

101
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
It has no result text

102
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
because the run never reached a clean answer.

103
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
But, and this matters,

104
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
it still carries full cost and usage accounting.

105
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
So even a run that blew its turn cap

106
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
tells you what it spent.

107
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,000
The lesson is simple.

108
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Branch on the subtype first,

109
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
then read the result text only when you know it's a success.

110
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Here's a nuance that catches people in production.

111
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
What happens when a single-shot query hits its turn cap?

112
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
It first yields the error result message,

113
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
the one with the error subtype.

114
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Then it raises an error with text

115
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
like reached maximum number of turns.

116
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
That's deliberate, but if you're not ready for it,

117
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
the exception stops your program cold.

118
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
So wrap a single-shot run in try and accept

119
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
when you want to catch that limit and keep going.

120
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
A streaming input session behaves differently.

121
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It doesn't raise, it stays alive.

122
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
So you can inspect the result and send another message.

123
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Same underlying loop,

124
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
but the input mode changes how a limit

125
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,000
actually reaches your code.

126
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Once a run stops,

127
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
three fields help you understand what happened.

128
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
The first is stop reason.

129
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
It tells you why the model ended a given turn,

130
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
whether that's in turn for a normal finish,

131
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
max tokens if it ran out of room, or a refusal.

132
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
The second is numb turns.

133
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The count of turns the run actually took.

134
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Watch that number because a run

135
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
that keeps climbing toward your cap

136
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
is a sign the agent is churning.

137
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
The third is session ID.

138
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
It identifies the whole run so you can resume it later

139
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
or trace it when you're debugging.

140
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Together, these three turn a finished run

141
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
from a black box into something you can read,

142
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
audit, and pick back up. Let's recap.

143
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
The agent loop is a repeating cycle.

144
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Reason, call tools, read results, repeat, and return.

145
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Each part reports through a typed message,

146
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
and each turn is a single tool round trip.

147
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
One thing to watch, the loop has no cap unless you set one,

148
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
so max turns is your safety net.

149
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
That covers how the agent loop runs under query.

150
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

